   "thinking": "Excellent! Now I have a very clear picture of what's happening. Let me analyze the logs:\n\n1. From `clickstream-ingest-6f9d8c7b4-2kqvx` (one of the original 4 pods):\n   - `consumer clickstream-raw: assigned partitions [0,1,2,3] (8 members in group clickstream-ingest)` - This pod has ALL 4 partitions [0,1,2,3] assigned to it, even though there are 8 members in the group.\n   - Wait, that's odd. If there are 8 members and only 4 partitions, normally each original member would have 1 partition and 4 new members would be idle. \n\nThat seems strange though, since with 8 consumers and only 4 partitions, I'd expect each consumer to get at most one partition, leaving the other four idle \u2014 not one consumer getting all four.\n\nActually, the more telling signal is on the producer side: the produce throttle_time is sitting around 2.8-2.9 seconds and the producer queue is 96% full, which strongly suggests the real bottleneck isn't consumer assignment at all, but the broker throttling the producer.\n\nThis points to a quota being enforced on their Kafka client API key by the data platform team, limiting producer throughput. Since the consumer-side partition count is also capped at 4, adding more replicas won't help on either front \u2014 it would just add more throttled producers rather than fixing the actual bottleneck.\n\nThis confirms the broker quota is owned by the data platform team, not our team, so we can't just change it ourselves. The evidence is clear: 96% full producer queue, 2.8-3s throttle times, explicit THROTTLE messages, and growing lag despite 8 pods running with very low CPU usage\u2014all pointing to the broker-side quota as the real bottleneck rather than anything on our scaling side.\n\nSince the fix requires either raising the producer quota or increasing partitions on clickstream-raw, this is fundamentally a coordination issue with the data platform team rather than something fixable through our own cluster config. I can close this